{
  "title": "Strategy for shared business logic between web and iOS clients",
  "description": "Business logic is currently re-implemented per client and \
drifts: recipe scaling (ramekin-ui/src/utils/scaleAmount.ts vs \
ramekin-ios/Ramekin/RecipeScaleSupport.swift), tag hierarchy parsing \
(utils/tagHierarchy.ts vs Shared/TagHierarchySupport.swift), ingredient \
display formatting (IngredientFormatting.swift vs web inline), meal plan date \
helpers, shopping list category ordering, etc.\n\
\n\
This is a research/decision task, not a big-bang refactor. For each piece of \
duplicated logic, decide among:\n\
\n\
1. Push to the server. Best where the data already round-trips through an \
endpoint (categories already work this way; category order could too — see \
p3-shopping-list-category-order-from-api). Doesn't fit interactive client-side \
things like live recipe scaling.\n\
2. Share code across clients. Most plausible shapes: (a) compile shared Rust \
(ramekin-core already has parsing/categorization logic) to WASM for web, and \
to a static lib or via UniFFI for iOS; (b) a shared TypeScript/JS core used \
directly on web and via JavaScriptCore on iOS. Evaluate build-system cost \
honestly — this repo already has heavy codegen, and another toolchain step has \
real maintenance weight.\n\
3. Accept duplication but pin it down with shared test vectors: a checked-in \
JSON file of input/expected pairs (scaling, tag parsing, formatting) that both \
the TS tests and Swift tests consume, so divergence fails CI instead of being \
discovered by users.\n\
\n\
Deliverable: a doc (doc/ or docs/agent/plans/) with a per-logic-area \
recommendation and follow-up issues for the chosen moves. Option 3 may be the \
right cheap default for most areas even if 1/2 are adopted selectively.",
  "status": "open",
  "priority": 3,
